{"verdict":"incorrect","findings":[{"id":"reviewer#1","title":"Subtract allowed lateness from the watermark","comment":"With any positive `allowed_lateness`, this adds the grace period to the watermark instead of subtracting it. For example, after tenant `acme` sends `ts=100` with lateness 30, an in-order reading at `ts=110` is counted as late and dropped because `110 < 130`; duplicate timestamps and every normal advance smaller than 30 seconds are lost as well. Use `watermark - self.allowed_lateness` so only readings farther behind the watermark than the configured grace period are rejected.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.EYSfVB/windows.py","lines":"70-71","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#2","title":"Retire accumulated windows when flushing a batch","comment":"`flush()` now returns a snapshot but never clears `_pending`. When `summarize_files` processes two or more batches, every later batch repeats all earlier windows, readings in the same tenant/window are emitted again as cumulative totals, and memory grows with the entire backfill rather than one batch. Clear `_pending` after constructing the returned rows, preserving `snapshot()` as the non-retiring operation.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.EYSfVB/aggregate.py","lines":"87","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#3","title":"Load tenant configuration outside the per-reading loop","comment":"Every reading calls `load_tenant_limits`, which opens and parses the same `tenants.json` file on each invocation. Large telemetry batches therefore perform one filesystem read and JSON parse per record—for example, a million-reading backfill performs a million identical config loads—so configuration lookup can dominate ingest time. Load the mapping once per summarization run or cache lookups for the run's config path.","priority":"P2","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.EYSfVB/pipeline.py","lines":"40","sources":["reviewer"]}],"lenses":[],"target":{"base":"main","baseCommit":"6376ffd22d58f2b7ed9ab94a449b8ab96cb75709","headCommit":"6074bc567799a38701b8dec61bf446a8a022f20b","repo":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.EYSfVB"},"level":"medium","coverage":[{"finder":"reviewer","lens":null,"status":"ok","candidates":3}],"explanation":"incorrect — Positive lateness currently drops valid in-order readings, and batch flushes retain and re-emit prior aggregates. The new tenant-limit lookup also introduces per-record file I/O that materially degrades large backfills."}
